well I think it's very very clear that  we need a wider array of more effective  treatments for PTSD no question  MDMA was actually first synthesized and  patented by merck pharmaceutical he  synthesized it in 1912 and patented in  1914 so but they never really did  anything with it and then in the 70s  alexander sasha shogun made some and  tried it experimented with it and he and  Dave Nichols published the first report  of human experience with MDMA and out of  that grew the interest you know he  thought immediately this is probably  could be useful as a therapeutic tool  but there were quite a number of  psychologists psychiatrists other  therapists using it kind of as a  catalyst for therapy in this late 70s  early 80s it was not a improved drug but  it wasn't illegal either in 82 I came  out to study with Stan Grof and  Christina Groff at Esalen and somebody  came to the month-long workshop with  MDMA and at first I was unimpressed the  way they described it because I thought  it's not the the bells and whistles and  the whole lightening and all this from  Millis tea or anything it was just  quietly helps you to talk to each other  and feel your emotions and I was like I  feel fine they're ready what I need it  for but I thought I should buy some  anyway and give it a try so I tried MDMA  with my girlfriend and it was just a  revelation about how profound it is and  at the same time I realized that it was  starting to be sold as ecstasy so I and  it was also the rise of the Nancy Reagan  just say no so I knew that it was  inevitably going to be crushed so in 84  I started a non-profit before Maps that  was the whole community together the the  therapists and the researchers and and  so we had a chance where it was legal to  try to build a base of support and  witnesses for a potential legal case and  that's where I contacted Robert Mueller  at the United Nations  the assistant secretary-general and he  helped me to meet a lot of religious  professionals who I sent MDMA to and  they tried it in monasteries and all  these places and and so we ended up in -  in 1984 the DEA moved to criminalize  MDMA and so we I went to Washington and  filed for an administrative law judge  hearing which to our utter astonishment  we won and the judge said that MDMA  should stay in schedule 3 it should be  illegal for non-medical use but  therapists and psychiatrists should be  able to have it available to their  patients but then the DEA administrator  overruled that and put it in schedule 1  so that's where I started maps in 86 and  the idea was that there is no way but  through the FDA that the drug war is too  powerful but maybe through science and  medicine we can make some progress it's  kind of an epidemic of PTSD now with all  the veterans coming back from Iraq and  Afghanistan to say nothing of all the  countries where there's armed conflict  and even higher levels of PTSD so it's a  huge public health problem they're  desperate last year was about  five-and-a-half billion dollars that the  VA spent on disability payments for  veterans with PTSD as a veteran in  Vietnam 65 66 out of the Huey battalion  and I came back in a pretty good case in  Postbank Vestas disorder and it was  really hard for a long time holding jobs  relationships and stuff and I saw this  they going to CNN about using some of  the psychedelics to relieve the symptoms  and I did it and it was wonderful it  really helped help me get my feet back  on the ground from a kind of a  therapeutic point of view what we think  it it seems to do is kind of decrease  fear and defensiveness anxiety increase  a sense of trust but also help people  connect with their emotions I think the  National Geographic had a great analogy  it's like  you threw a house and all the Glock  closets are open there's nothing you  can't explore in your own psyche and  it's just like magic but the wonderful  thing you don't have to keep taking it  for the rest of your life our first  study that 